message.content:
Today is October 2026. I'm checking novelty before building a lossless speculative-decoding method. Observation: draft acceptance is independent of serving concurrency because concurrent requests are independent. But in several important workloads the concurrent requests are CORRELATED: GRPO/RL rollout groups (G samples of the same prompt), best-of-N / self-consistency / parallel test-time scaling (n samples per question), tree-of-thought / agent fan-out sharing a context, batched templated jobs. Idea: a drafter (neural, e.g. DFlash/DSpark/EAGLE-style, or hybrid neural+retrieval) that conditions on the CONCURRENT SIBLING streams (their tokens and/or target hidden states / KV), so acceptance — and the useful draft length — RISE with the number of correlated requests in flight. Losslessness is kept by standard verification.

Search thoroughly (arXiv 2024–Oct 2026, OpenReview incl. ICLR 2027 submissions, MLSys/ASPLOS/OSDI/SOSP/EuroSys/NeurIPS/ICML/ACL 2025–26, GitHub: vLLM, SGLang, verl, OpenRLHF, NeMo-RL, slime, AReaL; blogs) for prior or concurrent work on:
1. speculative decoding that drafts from OTHER concurrent requests / sibling samples / rollout groups (model-free or neural): e.g. DAS (2511.13841), RhymeRL, SPEC-RL, FastGRPO, ReSpec (2510.26475), TLT (2511.16665), SuffixDecoding's global tree, "group speculative decoding", "cross-request speculation", "multi-sample speculative decoding", "speculative decoding for parallel sampling / best-of-N / self-consistency", "consensus drafting", "sibling drafting", "rollout reuse";
2. neural drafters with cross-sequence attention (draft head attending to other sequences in the batch, shared draft trees across requests, batch-level draft models);
3. analyses of how similar parallel samples / rollouts of the same prompt are (n-gram overlap, prefix sharing, divergence position) and how that changes with temperature;
4. related non-SD work that exploits sibling similarity (shared-prefix attention like Hydragen/cascade inference, rollout dedup/caching, "lookahead" from siblings).

For each relevant item: title, arXiv id + month (or PR/repo + date), venue if any, what exactly it does (model-free vs neural; uses past/completed rollouts vs concurrent in-flight siblings; same prompt vs different prompts), measured acceptance/speedup, batch/concurrency evaluated, and how close it is to the idea (identical / overlapping / adjacent). Verify ids on arxiv.org. End with a 4-sentence verdict: what is already done, and what precisely remains novel (e.g. neural sibling conditioning, in-flight vs completed siblings, inference-time best-of-N vs RL, acceptance rising with group size). Be concise; do not write files.

